# Prettier already reads .gitignore, so node_modules, frontend/dist and
# backend/public are covered there. They are repeated here so this file stands
# on its own if that default ever changes.
**/node_modules
frontend/dist
backend/public

# Generated by scripts/build-content.mjs from content/questions.source.json.
# Reformatting either one would make `npm run content:check` report drift
# against a source file that did not actually change.
frontend/src/content/questions.json
backend/deck.json

# npm writes these, npm owns their shape. Reformatting a lockfile only
# guarantees the next `npm install` fights the formatter.
**/package-lock.json

# Terraform has its own formatter — `terraform fmt` — and Prettier cannot
# parse HCL anyway.
infra/

# Browser-automation scratch output.
.playwright-mcp/

# The design language reference sits in the app root as one packed 10MB page
# with a single 9.5MB line. It is somebody's deliverable, not source: never
# reformatted, and Prettier chokes on it anyway. Root level only — the leading
# slash is what keeps this from matching frontend/index.html.
/*.html

# Markdown, deliberately, and this is the one exclusion that is a judgement
# call rather than a fact about the file.
#
# What formatting the docs bought was table-pipe alignment. What it cost was a
# collision with every long-lived branch at once: this repository runs a dozen
# agent branches, several of them old, and every one of them touches README.md
# or a spec. Padding every table in the repo puts a conflict in the path of all
# of them simultaneously, and puts it in the same files as their real work,
# where it makes the real conflict harder to read.
#
# It also quietly binds every future documentation edit to Prettier's idea of
# table padding, which is a workflow nobody asked for and which the writing in
# here does not need.
#
# Code stays formatted. Code is where a formatter settles arguments that are
# otherwise settled by whoever saved last; prose is not, and these tables were
# readable before.
*.md
